        "original_id": "ccffcadc-d9f9-5b10-a8f0-f113f0695269",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: George Lashwood, Beau Brummell, Edward George Wright, Prince Regent, King George IV\n- Places: England, London East End, Blackpool\n- Roles: English singer, music hall performer, fashion arbiter\n- Topics: Edwardian era, Regency England, men's fashion, music halls\n\nFacts:\n- George Bryan \"Beau\" Brummell was born on 7 June 1778 and died on 30 March 1840.\n- Beau Brummell was an iconic figure in Regency England, an arbiter of men's fashion, and a friend of the Prince Regent, the future King George IV.\n- George Lashwood was born as Edward George Wright on 25 April 1863 and died on 20 January 1942.\n- George Lashwood was a popular English singer of the Edwardian era who performed in music halls across England, especially in London's East End and at seaside venues such as Blackpool.\n- George Lashwood was known as \"the Beau Brummell of the music halls.\"",
